  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over\", gives the same answer on two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. Reply A is the string: ```json\\n{\"a\":1} {\\\"b\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\"c\":3}. Reply B is the string: {\"a\":1} {\\\"b\\\":2} {\\\"c\\\":3}. For Reply A, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\\\"b\\\":2}, because the fence holds two objects and the last one is {\\\"b\\\":2}; the function instead passes over the fenced candidate (the whole fence content is not a single JSON object) and returns the last top-level object {\\\"c\\\":3}. For Reply B, the rule requires the last top-level object {\\\"c\\\":3}, and the function returns {\\\"c\\\":3}. Thus both replies get the same answer {\\\"c\\\":3}, even though the rule requires {\\\"b\\\":2} for Reply A and {\\\"c\\\":3} for Reply B. What would make me wrong: if recover_json_object on Reply A actually returns {\\\"b\\\":2} (for example because the fenced pool is parsed object-by-object rather than as one candidate), or if the documented rule is read as requiring {\\\"c\\\":3} for Reply A because no single scoreable object is inside the fence, then the pair is not a violation.",
